The Bash, Zsh, Fish, and PowerShell completion scripts the completion plugin
prints, in dist/src/completion/bash.js, zsh.js, fish.js, and powershell.js, are
ported from these Cobra files:

- bash_completionsV2.go
- zsh_completions.go
- fish_completions.go
- powershell_completions.go

Changes made to the Cobra scripts:

- Every eval is removed. The program is called with an argument array, so a
  typed word reaches it as its characters and pressing Tab never runs it.
- Every construct that expands an operand is removed, Bash's compgen -W
  included. Offered words are filtered by string comparison.
- Quoting is removed without evaluation or expansion: by a string-only decoder
  in the Bash script, written for Bash 3.2, by the (Q) flag in Zsh, and in
  Fish by its own tokenizer (commandline -opc) for the words before the cursor
  and by string unescape --style=script for the word under it. When the word
  under the cursor holds an unclosed quote, the script offers nothing and does
  not call the program.
- The request carries every word before the cursor and the word under it, cut
  at the cursor, so no empty word is appended afterward.
- A nonzero exit, or an answer whose last line is not ":" followed by decimal
  digits, offers nothing. An error directive offers nothing, file names
  included.
- ActiveHelp handling is removed, including the ActiveHelp environment variable
  on the Fish and PowerShell requests.
- The application name enters a script only as data: as a single-quoted string
  in the shell's own quoting, as the suffix of function and variable
  identifiers in which
  every character outside A-Z, a-z, and 0-9 is encoded by its code point, and
  in the Zsh #compdef line and autoload check as it is.
- Offered values already carry the option's lead, such as --format=, so no
  script adds a flag prefix, and the Bash script no longer trims the current
  word after "=".
- The Bash script's dequoter reads $'...' as a "$" and a single-quoted string,
  so such a word reaches the program with its escapes undecoded. Nothing in it
  is evaluated.
- Bash escapes every offered word with printf %q, and every "~" too, which the
  printf of Bash 3.2 leaves bare and Bash expands after "=" or ":" in a word
  shaped like an assignment. With several matches each entry holds its
  word escaped, so the common prefix readline inserts is escaped text, and the
  list shows the escaped words.
- Bash calls no compgen -f or compgen -d, because in a completion Bash 3.2
  expands the directory part of their word. Without _filedir, the script lists
  file names by pathname expansion of the dequoted word with only a trailing
  "*" unquoted, so nothing typed is expanded or run and a $HOME or "~" in the
  word is never rewritten. It escapes each name itself rather than setting
  -o filenames.
- Bash without the bash-completion package builds its words from COMP_WORDS the
  way _get_comp_words_by_ref -n =: does, and honors directives 8 and 16 with
  that file listing and a string comparison of file extensions. The extension
  list is quoted when it is passed to _filedir.
- Bash without compopt, such as Bash 3.2, registers without -o default, so an
  error or a failed call never falls through to file names. The script
  completes file names itself with that file listing, only when the directive
  allows files and no word was offered. Bash with compopt keeps Cobra's
  registration.
- Zsh no longer splits words with ${=...}, passes _describe its arguments as an
  array, calls _files directly rather than through an _arguments action
  string, and quotes each file extension as a pattern with the (b) flag.
- Zsh escapes "\" and then ":" in each word and description it passes to
  _describe, which reads a backslash as an escape.
- Zsh sends $PREFIX, the word under the cursor cut at the cursor, rather than
  the whole word under the cursor.
- Zsh names its completion function _loom_<identifier> and its debug function
  __loom_<identifier>_debug, so no application name shadows a completion system
  function such as _describe, _files, or _arguments. The file still runs the
  function when compinit autoloads it as _<name>.
- Fish filters by prefix with a string comparison, keeps trailing empty lines
  in the answer, and offers nothing rather than file names after an error. As
  in Cobra's script, Fish completes every file name under directives 8 and 16.
- Directive numbers are written as literals, and comments are adjusted.
- PowerShell's Invoke-Expression is removed. The program is resolved with
  Get-Command, an application alone off Windows. On Windows it is called
  through & with an argument array. On PowerShell 7 off Windows, & expands a
  "~" and a wildcard in every word an array passes to a program, so there the
  script starts the program through ProcessStartInfo with each word in its
  ArgumentList, reads its output as UTF-8, and reads its stderr to the end and
  discards it. A program that resolves to a .bat or .cmd file is never called,
  because cmd.exe reads its arguments again as commands, and offers nothing.
- The PowerShell completer registers with -Native and reads its request from
  the elements of the command PowerShell's parser hands it, placed by their
  offsets: a constant string passes its value, any other element, such as a
  variable or a subexpression, the text typed, and the word under the cursor
  is its text cut at the cursor and read again by the parser, which runs
  nothing. A word that does not parse, such as one holding an unclosed quote,
  offers nothing.
- On Windows, under Legacy argument passing, which Cobra detected to pass an
  empty last word, the PowerShell script escapes every word: an empty word as "", and
  each " with a backslash, the backslashes before it doubled. The Windows
  style counts as Legacy for the files and commands PowerShell passes Legacy
  arguments to.
- On Windows, the PowerShell script reads the program's output as UTF-8 and
  restores the last exit code after the call. A failure of any kind, an error directive, or
  a directive that allows no files with no word offered returns one empty
  string, which keeps PowerShell from completing file names.
- The PowerShell script filters by an ordinal string comparison rather than
  -like, removes the directive line by its position, and no longer handles a
  flag with an equal sign. Its PSReadLine mode switch is removed: each word is
  one CompletionResult whose text is the word, bare when it holds only ASCII
  letters, digits, and - _ . / : =, and otherwise single-quoted with each '
  and each of U+2018 through U+201B written twice. Its list text is the word
  and its tooltip the description. A sole word takes a space after it, as in
  Cobra's Complete mode, unless directive 2 is set; no description is written
  into the text, and no other word takes a space.
- The PowerShell script honors directives 8 and 16 by listing the folder that
  the word under the cursor names, read literally, rather than completing every
  file name.
- Each script refuses an application name outside the portable name set.
